.TH CVEC_FIND 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_find \- find the first element with a given value (type-inferred)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

size_t idx = cvec_find(v, elem);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a type-inferred wrapper for cvector_find(). It accepts a value,
and an rvalue or a literal is also a value. The macro converts the needle
to the declared element type of the vector. It compares the elements with
the default comparison procedure of that type. Equality is therefore the
equality of the type and not the equality of its object representation.
chashmap and cbstmap use the same idea of equality for a key of the same
type. For example, -0.0 and 0.0 are one value. A long double compares by
value, and not through the padding bytes that its representation carries on
some targets.

A char*, signed char* or unsigned char* element (uint8_t* is an
unsigned char*) is a NUL-terminated string to this library, and the
comparison reads it with strcmp(). Do not search a vector of pointers to
binary buffers this way: a buffer with no NUL byte is read past its end.
Search such a vector with cvector_find(3) and a comparator that compares
the pointers, or declare the element type as void*, whose byte-wise
equality is the identity of the pointer.

An element type that is an array, such as char[64], takes an array or a
string literal as elem. The macro zero-fills its copy to the element size
and copies the bytes of elem into it, up to the smaller of the two sizes,
exactly as cvec_push(3) stores such a value. A pointer as elem for an
array element type is a compile error, because the macro cannot see the
size of what it points at.

A char* element can be NULL, and the needle can also be NULL. The default
string comparison procedure puts NULL before every string that is not NULL.
NULL is equal only to another NULL. A search for NULL in a sparse char*
vector therefore gives the index of the first empty slot. The macro
dereferences nothing.

Some element types have no default comparison procedure. These are a bool,
a struct, a union, a pointer to anything other than char, and a fixed-size
char array. For such a type, the comparison falls back to byte-wise
equality over the whole representation of the element, and that includes
the padding bytes of a struct. With GCC 11 or later, the macro sets the
padding bytes of its own copy of elem to zero. cvec_push(3) does the
same for each element that it stores. The macro therefore finds a
struct that went in through cvec_push(3) with any value whose members
are equal. The padding bytes of that value have no effect. An element that went
in through cvector_push_back(3), cvector_append_array(3) or an assignment
through cvec_at(3) keeps the padding bytes that the caller wrote. With a
compiler that offers no __builtin_clear_padding, such as Clang, the
padding bytes of the macro copies are unspecified. Search for a padded
struct with cvector_find(3) and a comparator that reads the members.
cvec_find compiles for every element type. cvec_sort is deliberately
different. An ordering cannot come from the bytes of an element in the way that equality can.
cvec_sort therefore has no fallback and rejects such an element type at
compile time.

Use cvector_find() directly when you need a custom comparator. Use it also
when you want byte-wise equality for a type that has a default comparison
procedure.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v     Vector to search
elem  Element value to search for. It can be an rvalue or an lvalue.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Zero-based index of the first element that matches.
ccol_invalid_size when there is no match.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(n) complexity
The macro compares with the default comparison procedure of the element
type.
The macro orders a NULL char* element, and a NULL needle for a char*
vector. It dereferences neither of them.
The macro falls back to byte-wise equality for a type that has no default
comparison procedure. A struct with padding bytes compares correctly only
when both copies carry zero padding. See DESCRIPTION.
The macro compiles for every element type. cvec_sort does not.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 10);
cvec_push(vec, 20);
cvec_push(vec, 30);
size_t idx = cvec_find(vec, 20);   // idx == 1
size_t nf  = cvec_find(vec, 99);   // nf  == ccol_invalid_size
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_find (3),
.BR cvec_push (3),
.BR cvec_sort (3)
